431	/* The general form of the same fix, for every file this pass did not open.
432	   `text-white`/`bg-white`/`border-white` (bare, 100% opacity) are headings and
433	   want ink — that is what flipping the token above does. Anything with a
434	   slash after "white" is an OPACITY variant — `white/10`, `white/[0.04]`,
435	   `hover:bg-white/20` — and those want a wash relative to whatever the
436	   surface actually is now, not a tint of ink-through-transparency. Matched on
437	   the raw class attribute text, so it does not matter which prefix (hover:,
438	   sm:, etc.) precedes the utility. Gradient stop utilities (from-white/N,
439	   via-white/N, to-white/N) are not covered — those set a custom property
440	   rather than a plain CSS property, so there is no single declaration to
441	   override this way; decorative sheens, not text, so lower cost left as is. */
442	/* Islands are EXCLUDED at the selector, not overridden after the fact —
443	   `revert` does not mean "the value this same rule would have skipped", it
444	   rolls back to a different cascade origin entirely, so it cannot undo an
445	   `!important` sibling rule. `:not()` accepts a full selector list including
446	   descendant combinators, so this reads as "matches, and is not the island and
447	   is not inside one" in one step. Inside dark chrome, "white at 10% opacity on
448	   dark" is already correct in both themes and must stay untouched. */
